Trie advantages: inserts/finds traverse a path matching the word, sharing prefixes reduces duplication; autocomplete across a prefix becomes a subtree traversal, yielding all completions efficiently. Hash maps store exact keys; prefix queries require scanning all keys or maintaining inverted indexes, which is costly. Trade-offs: Tries incur higher pointer/structural overhead per node and can be memory-heavy with large alphabets or sparse usage; compression (Patricia/radix) or fixed-size arrays can mitigate this. Deletion is non-trivial due to shared prefixes. Real-world nuance: store frequencies at nodes to surface top-k suggestions, or prune with a capped DFS for responsive UI.

